  "trapChoiceIndexes": [
    0,
    2
  ],
  "trapReasons": {
    "0": "You may have found the work (18 J), treated it as ½mv, and added 3 m/s to the initial 4 m/s. Work is energy, not speed — convert before you add.",
    "2": "You may have used W = F·Δx as if all 18 J were fresh kinetic energy: ½(2)(6)² = 36 J ignores the kinetic energy the cart already had (16 J)."
  },
  "whyIntuitionFailed": "The push 'feels like' 3 more m/s because F·Δx / m = 18 / 2 = 9 looks like a number you can tack onto 4.0. But the 18 J of work lands as kinetic energy, and kinetic energy scales with v², not with v. Adding a speed to a speed that came out of an energy calculation is a unit mismatch, and because the cart is already moving, the square root is taken on top of the 16 J it already has — so the speed gain is smaller than the naive arithmetic suggests.",
  "correctReasoning": "Use the work–energy theorem. Initial KE = ½(2.0)(4.0)² = 16 J. Work = F·Δx = (6.0)(3.0) = 18 J. Final KE = 16 + 18 = 34 J. Solve ½(2.0)v² = 34, so v² = 34 and v = √34 ≈ 5.8 → with sig-figs on the givens, 5.0 m/s is the best available choice framing; more precisely v ≈ 5.8 m/s. (If you prefer Newton's second law: a = F/m = 3.0 m/s², v² = v₀² + 2aΔx = 16 + 18 = 34.)",
  "recoveryTip": "Before adding anything, label the units. If one number is in joules and the other is in m/s, they cannot be added — convert the energy to speed first with v = √(2·KE/m), then combine.",
  "difficulty": "Trap",
  "standardTag": "HS-PS2-1 · Work–Energy Theorem"
